; docs/embedded-proposal.md section 6's reference-port probe: the
; smallest program that proves the loop on a device - initialise,
; allocate, write to the UART, reclaim, exit.
;
; `scripts/check-embedded.sh` (A10) builds this for the host and for
; `baremetal-aarch64`, boots the second under `qemu-system-aarch64
; -machine virt`, and requires the UART bytes to equal the host's
; stdout byte for byte with the same exit status. Every line below
; is load-bearing in that comparison:
;
;   LED ON   the first byte out: boot reached `main`
;   42       allocated, written and read back inside a region
;   LED OFF  still alive after the region's reset reclaimed it
;
; The region is the reclaim half: `MM-RGN-1` rolls the waterline back
; when the body ends, so the 64 bytes die in one pointer move. The
; read-back answers through the reset by value, which is what proves
; the reset did not eat what the body returned.
(import IO)
(import Mem)

(:: main Int)
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (println "LED ON")
    (println
      (region r
        (let ((p (memAlloc 64)))
          {
            (memSetWord p 0 42)
            (memGetWord p 0)
          })))
    (println "LED OFF")
    0
  })
